#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124} :activity/id #uuid "ffe0a9ff-b89d-5315-a9ba-afafd34b3fa7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153391 :tutorial/content "We know how to define the double integral of a function over a rectangular domain. Now, we'll learn how to define a double integral over a non-rectangular region.\n\nLet's consider the non-rectangular region $D$ enclosed by the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e80bcbaff16282f00e7e3df487d78cfbc51bb2e9660dde5e2fc421cd4d70a9af.png)\n\nWe will define the following double integral over this non-rectangular region: $$\\iint\\limits_{D} (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A$$\n\nTo do this, we will use the following trick. Let's enclose the region $D$ by a rectangle $R$ with sides parallel to the coordinates axes, as shown below.\n\n![](images/21/214db95d949a8658e3fdbfea8f54c0e3bf914c11d9ef88e1ca4264d64d360c90.png)\n\nNow, we extend our function to $R$ by setting a new function $f(x,y)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $D$ and equals $3-x^2-2y^2$ on $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$f(x,y) = {\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$$\n\n![](images/43/43288ccc496832a1a78f9fcaaabb4a5841fc79db5d2082dff7d4177005b61d01.png)\n\nAlthough $f(x,y)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $D,$ the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over the rectangular domain $R$ will exist.\n\nTherefore, we define our double integral of $3-x^2-2y^2$ over $D$ as\n\n$$\\iint\\limits_D (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A.$$\n\nGeometrically, the value of the double integral will give us the volume of the solid bounded by the surface $z=3-x^2-2y^2$ over the quarter-circular domain $D.$\n\n**Watch out!** The domain $R$ must not necessarily be the smallest possible rectangle that covers the region $D.$ It can be any rectangular domain that covers $D$ completely." :tutorial/id #uuid "14a20ee8-f248-507f-bce6-8dc6be91b9e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5df5591-1e92-560d-ba7c-bbf286cf8b6b" :step/math-academy-id 25787 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152005 :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9b86ebc6-eb40-565f-a521-f246f439bdd2" :step/math-academy-id 25788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380824}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "a01eeffe-e0b1-5e2a-be46-b8854fed7312" :step/math-academy-id 25790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152007 :question/id #uuid "fe8eb478-6830-55bc-84c6-668924daaefc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6997"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425931}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "3132ab29-3ec9-50e2-aca1-43d58e9447c0" :step/math-academy-id 25792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153125}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152328}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407300} :activity/id #uuid "b8cf7360-113f-5967-a53f-923c8be1dfcf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23853c33-d0ed-571b-9440-f3644fa48df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345675} {:answer/id #uuid "3e36c1ae-af25-5605-87dc-f2ece1a31876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345878} {:answer/id #uuid "84837529-249c-5b0e-9cd2-843e73c7c6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346506} {:answer/id #uuid "b61b47b3-7047-55ed-9f36-f1cf4c9519b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346937} {:answer/id #uuid "d24b6135-f0aa-5dca-ba04-518c8974508c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346937} :answer-field/id #uuid "0220025d-38d4-5517-bac4-a948b572b394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8098b383-f4a9-5d0e-9352-b359bad47678" :question/math-academy-id "q-80430" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "080248f8-ed70-5a9f-8bf1-a2b4de4eb4f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197936d1-4a7e-51b8-91f0-94b31cb7063c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407278} {:answer/id #uuid "4012db07-82ce-5c39-93dc-c15349b6c4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407281} {:answer/id #uuid "69f11751-9b65-5442-9be4-dfc89e4dd373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407285} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce4e9eb-5239-59bf-9aa9-acfacda1c294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407288} {:answer/id #uuid "f33c6146-83ba-5ed5-bb69-2dc2054153f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407285} :answer-field/id #uuid "785531fb-5cc7-5887-a5ee-25914bfbfc99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04b43074-9710-54c1-a656-7d7797008d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-129786" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d66cb97b9e04367ce2250e5156eaa9daaf42fadb395220e0464bca687e91908.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$\n- ${∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "30bd25e3-ac80-5e5b-b4e0-5224a24a94ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160b1926-1bcd-5c5b-bd7d-bf6e5649f985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345543} {:answer/id #uuid "305027c1-06e6-56de-a556-2b5230d47d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345779} {:answer/id #uuid "804f49a5-d816-55a4-a4ff-34a63af08cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346473} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f9c0f2-e674-513c-82fb-0d2f5a40569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346789} {:answer/id #uuid "f082cd67-0546-5f90-8651-029089a3b6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0749705b-ee1b-5d79-b079-3b1d50ac9f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ca2c9d4-83c1-5596-b062-1537df902124" :question/math-academy-id "q-80425" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d09a23a1ae9f737c7f19ba83d6d20c3fbcea3c7deb71fa307264c90afabc6580.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/16/1620acdcfd3783df478322ebe5e81cb274d908db12d9c8bdc701fe21070b828f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4e94e485-56a2-5844-806a-2096ce99769f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "657bd7d3-dd1c-5d1e-a2e2-e5779123f72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407284} {:answer/id #uuid "784f60d8-744f-5a20-8590-4309ac0a7fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac4f900-28cf-5d47-aca1-6ede50a776f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407287} {:answer/id #uuid "c63263fe-1e9f-59ea-a5b6-858826246c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407289} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc6305c-bf3a-5c13-af4f-f56b112873c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407286} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e76b06d-2177-5f41-ae22-04ed4efb0ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed965e5b-f0df-5c0f-9c7c-0cb7a433c067" :question/math-academy-id "q-86866" :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "5f132e51-d1d4-5811-8af1-98e3b3699b32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0575dc8f-eac3-5b1e-a66b-25c751aa05e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407277} {:answer/id #uuid "1c78122a-3a25-5733-8658-cd93b68aea0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407279} {:answer/id #uuid "3c793446-7160-555d-ab53-99c4b0187b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407280} {:answer/id #uuid "4b681d8b-d313-54f5-a6b1-5e551fd84bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407282} {:answer/id #uuid "57aebbe5-21ba-5866-a272-d33fb018e22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407277} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a4c622-55f3-52fb-965d-fe7f4db5d8a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bbe5a871-1924-5655-ad62-5cf32208bd34" :question/math-academy-id "q-129908" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=2-|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1a/1a5369b58b14444c5ea0ce4bb36954d1d0b7964dcef4e95f9cc527694b3a9e4f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "98a2aaad-910d-5774-ad63-69bb719493bb"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425932} :activity/id #uuid "c1b2bf8a-6e24-5f4d-b8cf-9b6296b332cb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dc624a-6195-52a9-9917-f3684e4a574f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425913} {:answer/id #uuid "4a78a72b-b4d7-5293-8aba-7c35212132ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425914} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3e8aa2-20ed-5607-9c37-0e3dceaf9028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425915} {:answer/id #uuid "9f70c45e-aafb-568b-ad0e-18cd0c73be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425919} {:answer/id #uuid "f078a363-807c-53b3-b111-d4c6e7e91d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425915} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a9dd81-ee99-5b34-b7e2-398216f26e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19ad1761-3136-5be8-b038-5aa62265f48b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80526" :question/problem "![](images/88/8866056ce5c7c80ed8452246cc3a40074b6841615ed6ef472e7a1612709e51fa.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4e98cf8b-1d7c-55a2-90e4-587c132c726b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16080a41-2e2f-51fa-a9c4-12eb38603975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351762} {:answer/id #uuid "74bad7f9-9b45-59d6-9532-f075ca6e95ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352591} {:answer/id #uuid "8a40d22c-1bca-53d5-b4c4-4e579271dd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352772} {:answer/id #uuid "d925b33c-0df5-5c5b-9a60-d057d993edef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353455} {:answer/id #uuid "e226575c-1f65-5f96-b71a-27515a394a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351762} :answer-field/id #uuid "13869e20-bad1-5d54-bcbb-82ddee104a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa4fe275-03b1-57bc-8327-4434c307d887" :question/math-academy-id "q-86899" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-|x|+2$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "65ccb889-1cb9-5b78-a946-1218d327ccf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e995b4f-80df-5a44-ac52-ef3e8528fb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425916} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea449c7-e858-51b4-bdf2-6beadec281c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425918} {:answer/id #uuid "c738fcf4-81ab-523b-a4a3-3c52b0e51091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425920} {:answer/id #uuid "e52b4a76-97bf-58a6-8111-6703e511f2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425922} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb50184-5f7d-5a30-8c1d-9227e10a07fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425916} :answer-field/id #uuid "30306495-7925-535a-81a1-49a15747abcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1654ded7-c0bc-579c-a9e9-6daa6a1243e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86894" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d8f57afa16e99c13e6e36c5544a7c7594da13321bd3d01692a5a296a20953d7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "669eeb99-fe93-5bc0-a8ac-52fd38d35e53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b1980b-e87f-5251-b68d-f7b2a5280848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352187} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd2e348-e399-5345-96ea-c3ac2b25e252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352672} {:answer/id #uuid "a578f3f2-5df9-51d4-b3ac-e913a144510c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352977} {:answer/id #uuid "abf9fc1e-1781-5aab-b380-3abe698af5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353026} {:answer/id #uuid "bd863838-ce95-5779-b5d7-f63609482fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352672} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe6f8d1-0f2d-5248-98d5-e4c5850a61a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b045a52-4215-5bdb-9dcf-5641984e3591" :question/math-academy-id "q-86898" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eedab041-fe80-5aa7-8ce9-3144bd5d6aa6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034daae9-6479-5df1-9b65-7e6139e7a49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425911} {:answer/id #uuid "204a2417-b1ee-5a94-9387-92210e84c9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425912} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd19b67-41ce-5d20-9075-74172011db9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425917} {:answer/id #uuid "daf363de-8d31-5c81-8ebc-62bc6b37b91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425921} {:answer/id #uuid "fa68e695-7f74-50ec-acaf-b9b8df374c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425921} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b56bb6c-74dd-5bd4-a9cd-cbed30098ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c04d4c5-9913-59ea-a83f-46d8bb7c02cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-129893" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfc135232008824ae0522bcc8454c5f97c2dfd13fb6e333e146a040ccbc1f22.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statements II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f7de3cb3-0140-51ce-a3e4-58a349b9b853"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425910}]}
